\nL1036:   * [WEBIDL] defines the following terms:\nL1037:     * DOMString\nL1038:     * Exposed\nL1039:     * InvalidModificationError\nL1040:     * SyntaxError\nL1041:     * boolean\nL1042:     * this\nL1043:     * throw\nL1044:     * undefined\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCSS Values and Units Module Level 5 (https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/)\nciteturn1279view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"https://drafts.csswg.org/css-values-5/\",\"pattern\":\"CSS-wide keyword\"}); Total lines: 2842\nL795: All properties implicitly accept a cite58†<whole-value> as their entire value, just as they accept the cite490†CSS-wide keywords as their entire value.\nL796: \nL797: When used as a component value of a function, cite58†<whole-value> also represents any CSS value normally valid as the whole value of the property in which it is used (including additional <whole-value> functions). However, some functions may restrict what a <whole-value> argument can include.\nL850: If the result of `condition` is false, cite533†continue†infra.spec.whatwg.org .\nL851: \nL852:     3. cite177†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions in the second cite123†<declaration-value> of `branch`, and return the result.\nL853: \nL854:   2. Return nothing (an empty sequence of cite174†component values ).\nL855: Note: Unlike using cite136†@media /cite137†@supports /cite141†@container rules, which just ignore their contents when they’re false and let the cascade determine what values otherwise apply, declarations with cite121†if() do not roll back the cascade if the conditions are false; any fallback values must be provided inline. However, see the cite538†revert-rule cite490†CSS-wide keyword .\nL856: ### 8.4. Toggling Between Values: the cite539†cycle() notation\nL857: \nL858: The cycle() expression allows descendant elements to cycle over a list of values instead of inheriting the same value.\nL859: \nL860: The following example makes `<em>` elements italic in general, but makes them normal if they’re inside something that’s italic:\nL861: \nL862:     em { font-style: cycle(italic, normal); }\nL863: The following example cycles markers for nested lists, so that a top level list has cite540†disc -shaped markers, but nested lists use cite541†circle , then cite542†square , then box, and then repeat through the list of marker shapes, starting again (for the 5th list deep) with disc.\nL864: \nL865:     ul { list-style-type: cycle(disc, circle, square, box); }\nL866: \nL867: The syntax of the cite539†cycle() expression is:\nL868: \nL869:     cite539†<cycle()> = cycle( cite58†<whole-value> cite427†# )\nL956:   * cite588†attr-container-style-query.html†wpt.fyi cite589†(live test)†wpt.live cite590†(source)†github.com L957:   * cite591†attr-crash.html†wpt.fyi cite592†(live test)†wpt.live cite593†(source)†github.com L958:   * cite594†attr-css-wide-keywords.html†wpt.fyi cite595†(live test)†wpt.live cite596†(source)†github.com L959:   * cite597†attr-cycle.html†wpt.fyi cite598†(live test)†wpt.live cite599†(source)†github.com L960:   * cite600†attr-dynamic-marker-content.html†wpt.fyi cite601†(live test)†wpt.live cite602†(source)†github.com L1990:   * cite1091†variable-declaration-16.html†wpt.fyi cite1092†(live test)†wpt.live cite1093†(source)†github.com L1991:   * cite1094†variable-declaration-17.html†wpt.fyi cite1095†(live test)†wpt.live cite1096†(source)†github.com L1992:   * cite1097†variable-declaration-18.html†wpt.fyi cite1098†(live test)†wpt.live cite1099†(source)†github.com L1993:   * cite1100†variable-declaration-19.html†wpt.fyi cite1101†(live test)†wpt.live cite1102†(source)†github.com L1994:   * cite1103†variable-declaration-21.html†wpt.fyi cite1104†(live test)†wpt.live cite1105†(source)†github.com L1995:   * cite1106†variable-transitions-transition-property-all-before-value.html†wpt.fyi cite1107†(live test)†wpt.live cite1108†(source)†github.com L1996:   * cite1109†variable-transitions-value-before-transition-property-all.html†wpt.fyi cite1110†(live test)†wpt.live cite1111†(source)†github.com L1997: If a property value, after cite537†property replacement , contains only a single cite490†CSS-wide keyword (and possibly whitespace/comments), its value is determined as if that keyword were its cite399†specified value all along.\nL1998: \nL1999: Tests\nL2000:   * cite1112†whitespace-in-fallback-crash.html†wpt.fyi cite1113†(live test)†wpt.live cite1114†(source)†github.com L2001:   * cite1115†wide-keyword-fallback-001.html†wpt.fyi cite1116†(live test)†wpt.live cite1117†(source)†github.com L2002:   * cite1118†wide-keyword-fallback-002.html†wpt.fyi cite1119†(live test)†wpt.live cite1120†(source)†github.com L2003: For example, the following usage is fine from a syntax standpoint, but results in nonsense when the variable is substituted in:\nL2004: \nL2005:     :root { --looks-valid: 20px; }\nL2006:     p { background-color: var(--looks-valid); }\nL2007: \nL2008: \nL2009: Since 20px is an invalid value for cite1121†background-color , the property becomes cite492†invalid at computed-value time , and instead resolves to cite1122†transparent (the cite1123†initial value for background-color).\nL2010: If the property was one that’s inherited by default, such as cite1124†color , it would compute to the inherited value rather than the initial value.\nL2011: \nL2012: While a cite133†var() function can’t get a cite490†CSS-wide keyword from the cite548†custom property itself—​if you tried to specify that, like --foo: initial;, it would just trigger cite1125†explicit defaulting for the custom property—​it can have a CSS-wide keyword in its fallback:\nL2013: \nL2014:     p { color: var(--does-not-exist, initial); }\nL2015: In the above code, if the --does-not-exist property didn’t exist or is cite492†invalid at computed-value time , the cite133†var() will instead substitute in the cite1126†initial keyword, making the property behave as if it was originally cite1124†color: initial . This will make it take on the document’s initial color value, rather than defaulting to inheritance, as it would if there were no fallback.\nL2016: \nL2017: To replace substitution functions in a property `prop`:\nL2018:   1. cite177†Substitute arbitrary substitution functions in `prop`’s value, given «\"property\", `prop`’s name» as the cite535†substitution context . Let `result` be the returned cite174†component value sequence.\nL2019: \nL2020:   2. If `result` contains the cite175†guaranteed-invalid value , `prop` is cite492†invalid at computed-value time ; return.\nL2021: \nL2022:   3. cite166†Parse `result` according to `prop`’s grammar. If this returns failure, `prop` is cite492†invalid at computed-value time ; return.\nL2023:   4. Otherwise, replace `prop`’s value with the parsed result.\nL2024: \nL2025: Tests\nL2026:   * cite1127†css-variable-change-style-001.html†wpt.fyi cite1128†(live test)†wpt.live cite1129†(source)†github.com L2027:   * cite1130†css-variable-change-style-002.html†wpt.fyi cite1131†(live test)†wpt.live cite1132†(source)†github.com L2028:   * cite1133†variable-declaration-01.html†wpt.fyi cite1134†(live test)†wpt.live cite1135†(source)†github.com L2029:   * cite1136†variable-declaration-02.html†wpt.fyi cite1137†(live test)†wpt.live cite1138†(source)†github.com L2030:   * cite1139†variable-declaration-03.html†wpt.fyi cite1140†(live test)†wpt.live cite1141†(source)†github.com L2031:   * cite1142†variable-declaration-04.html†wpt.fyi cite1143†(live test)†wpt.live cite1144†(source)†github.com L2032:   * cite1145†variable-declaration-05.html†wpt.fyi cite1146†(live test)†wpt.live cite1